1
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:06,880
Hi everyone! This talk is about a device whose
active part is a single atom, one phosphorus

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:11,240
or arsenic atom in silicon, put there
with a scanning tunneling microscope.

3
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:16,220
Physically, that atom acts like a hydrogen
atom living inside a semiconductor.

4
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,000
One extra electron held by
a weakened Coulomb pull.

5
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:25,200
And in EE143 terms, every step
is a process we already know.

6
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:30,340
Resist, lithography, gas phase doping,
an anneal, epitaxy, etch and metal.

7
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:34,880
The difference is that the resist is one
atom thick, and the dose is one molecule.

8
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:41,920
Three ideas run through this
whole talk. One is precision.

9
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:47,960
One lattice site on the silicon surface is 0.38
nanometers, and that's the placement error

10
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:48,980
we can reach.

11
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:55,200
Two is yield. If each site works with probability
p, an array of n sites works with p to the n.

12
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:59,260
So p has to be really
close to 1. Three is speed.

13
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:03,200
A tip writes about 100 atoms per
second, and that sets the limit.

14
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:06,360
Act one is why one atom makes a good qubit.

15
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:10,380
Act two is how it's built.
Act three is does it work?

16
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:14,460
Act four is how it scales,
but keep one question in mind.

17
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:18,240
How do you put exactly one atom
exactly where you want it?

18
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:24,510
Think of the donor as
hydrogen inside a dielectric.

19
00:01:24,630 --> 00:01:28,510
The Bohr radius scales for the dielectric
constant times the free mass over the effective

20
00:01:28,530 --> 00:01:32,990
mass. Silicon has a big dielectric
constant and a small effective mass.

21
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:37,650
So hydrogen 0.05 nanometers
stretches to about two nanometers.

22
00:01:37,950 --> 00:01:41,250
You get two spins for free. The
electron and the phosphorus nucleus.

23
00:01:41,610 --> 00:01:43,730
Two times two gives a four level system.

24
00:01:43,910 --> 00:01:48,030
Electron spins are driven with
ESR, and nucleus spins with NMR.

25
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:52,870
Readout turns spin into charge. A spin up
electron can tunnel into a nearby single electron

26
00:01:52,890 --> 00:01:53,890
transistor.

27
00:01:54,290 --> 00:01:55,650
A spin down one can't.

28
00:01:56,110 --> 00:01:57,770
And the transistor current changes.

29
00:01:58,250 --> 00:02:03,350
In purified silicon 28, only about 200
parts per million of silicon 29 is left.

30
00:02:03,690 --> 00:02:09,390
So stray nuclear spins are rare. That's why
implanted donors there reach 35.6 seconds of

31
00:02:09,410 --> 00:02:13,230
coherence and gates near 99.99%.

32
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:16,610
Is it really hydrogen like you might ask?

33
00:02:16,630 --> 00:02:21,430
To first order, yes it is. Silicon's six
conduction band valleys split the ground state.

34
00:02:25,190 --> 00:02:30,330
Two qubit gates use exchange, called J, which
comes from the two electron wave functions

35
00:02:30,350 --> 00:02:31,350
overlapping.

36
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:34,250
The wave function squared
dies off exponentially.

37
00:02:34,870 --> 00:02:37,310
E to the minus two r over the Bohr radius.

38
00:02:37,810 --> 00:02:40,790
So the coupling drops exponentially
as the donors move apart.

39
00:02:41,430 --> 00:02:46,730
Let's do a quick estimate. Move one donor by
one lattice site and that term shrinks by E to

40
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:54,950
the minus 0.38, about 0.7. That's roughly a
30% change in coupling from a one site mistake.

41
00:02:55,590 --> 00:02:59,570
On top of that, silicon's valleys
make J jump around from site to site.

42
00:02:59,850 --> 00:03:01,710
So precision isn't optional.

43
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:05,870
The map is drawn to scale. Two
donors, 13 nanometers apart.

44
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:09,550
Now you might ask, can't
you just tune J with gates?

45
00:03:09,590 --> 00:03:13,650
Yes, you can, but only within a
window set by where the atoms are.

46
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:22,480
Ion implantation is random.
It's a Poisson process.

47
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:28,560
If the average number of ions per site is
lambda, the chance of exactly one is lambda times

48
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:33,860
e to the minus lambda. That peaks at lambda
equals one, where the chance is one over e,

49
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:40,240
about 37%. So without counting
ions, no dose ever beats 37%.

50
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:48,380
Those bars are that distribution, even with
the best ion counting, up to 99.85% confidence

51
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:55,320
at 14 kilo electron volts, each ion still
stops at a random spot, spread over nanometers.

52
00:03:56,240 --> 00:04:02,140
STM placement is locked to the lattice, plus
or minus one site, and arsenic windows give 97

53
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:03,800
% single atoms.

54
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:06,800
Now you might ask, what about lower energy?

55
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:11,400
Well, in that case, the spread shrinks,
but it never gets down to one lattice site.

56
00:04:17,860 --> 00:04:20,920
So here's the device as an EE spec sheet.

57
00:04:20,940 --> 00:04:22,820
So we have the substrate silicon,

58
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:29,260
purified silicon 28 for qubits, dopants,
phosphorus from phosphine, or arsenic from arsine.

59
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:33,480
Placement is plus or minus 0.38 nanometers.

60
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,040
The leads are doped sheets around 10 to the
14 per square centimeter, under one nanometer

61
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:41,700
thick. That's a 2D electron gas.

62
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:46,060
The Kane design on the right needs about
one Tesla, and about a tenth of a Kelvin.

63
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:50,680
That way the spin's Zeeman energy is way
bigger than the thermal energy, and the spin

64
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:51,780
starts polarized.

65
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:58,060
A gates tune the hyperfine, J gates tune the
exchange between donors about 10 nanometers apart.

66
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:01,640
But you might ask, why millikelvin?

67
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,260
Well, that's because thermal energy has to sit
far below the Zeeman splitting, so the spin

68
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:07,840
starts in a known state.

69
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:15,440
So, if we read the timeline

70
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:19,260
from a list of solved physics problems.

71
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:20,940
1994,

72
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:27,080
a tip strips hydrogen off silicon by
exciting the silicon hydrogen bond.

73
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:28,820
That's a lithography.

74
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:30,520
1998,

75
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:34,300
the proposal that donor
nuclear spins can be qubits.

76
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:35,800
2003,

77
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:38,400
single phosphorus atoms placed on purpose.

78
00:05:38,920 --> 00:05:41,980
2012, a transistor whose channel is one atom.

79
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:43,400
2019,

80
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:46,000
a fast exchange gate and 3D stacking.

81
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:53,700
2020 to 2024, arsenic chemistry pushes single
atom yield to 97%, and light starts replacing

82
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:55,600
the tip for coarse patterning.

83
00:05:56,320 --> 00:05:59,340
2025, 4 and 11 qubit processors.

84
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:03,960
So, we see here that there
is a same recipe every time.

85
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:05,820
Passivate, pattern,

86
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:08,180
dose, anneal, cap.

87
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:12,140
Now, you might ask, why did it take so long?

88
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:15,600
Well, each step needed a new way
to measure what was happening.

89
00:06:22,740 --> 00:06:29,720
Now, Dr. Neil Curson's group at UCL works
on the materials physics underneath this

90
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:32,500
platform. There's three threads to this.

91
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:37,660
One is dopant chemistry, so swapping phosphine
for arsine, which breaks apart about a

92
00:06:37,680 --> 00:06:38,940
thousand times faster.

93
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:44,800
In Arrhenius terms, that's like lowering the
barrier by kT times the log of a thousand,

94
00:06:45,080 --> 00:06:48,600
about 0.18 electron volts at room temperature.

95
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:55,100
Two, metrology, imaging buried layers with
x-ray fluorescence without destroying them and

96
00:06:55,120 --> 00:06:59,280
using photoemission to see electrons
squeezed into less than a nanometer.

97
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:05,580
Three is scaling, showing that extreme ultraviolet
light can strip the same hydrogen resist,

98
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:09,340
plus arsenic and germanium, which
goes in at room temperature.

99
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:14,680
So, why arsenic? Well, it's
because it breaks apart faster.

100
00:07:15,140 --> 00:07:18,200
It needs fewer free sites, so
the windows can be smaller.

101
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:23,240
So, here's the process.

102
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:28,880
So, we see here some
travelers that I will mention.

103
00:07:29,320 --> 00:07:32,100
So, the key role here is thermal history.

104
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:36,980
So, hot steps first, and then
everything at 500 degrees C or below.

105
00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:40,280
Steps one to three clean
and passivate the surface.

106
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:47,280
Step four is lithography. Steps five and six
are gas doping and activation at 350 degrees.

107
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:51,600
Steps seven and eight bury the
dopants with epitaxy at 250.

108
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:57,260
Steps nine to eleven are alignment,
etch, aluminum, contacts, and testing.

109
00:07:57,900 --> 00:07:59,180
The design rule is diffusion.

110
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:04,300
Diffusion length is two root dt, and d
goes up exponentially with temperature.

111
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:07,900
So, the hottest step after
patterning sets the whole budget.

112
00:08:08,900 --> 00:08:11,020
Now, which step limits throughput?

113
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:14,920
That is step four, which is the tip.

114
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:23,220
So, let's prep the surface.
So, how do we do that?

115
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:26,540
We out gas for over eight
hours at 600 degrees.

116
00:08:27,420 --> 00:08:29,320
Then, flash to about 1200.

117
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:31,820
That burns off the oxide and the carbon,

118
00:08:32,260 --> 00:08:34,880
and leaves clean rows of silicon dimers.

119
00:08:35,060 --> 00:08:38,900
The STM chamber sits below five
times 10 to the minus 11 millibar.

120
00:08:39,020 --> 00:08:44,040
Why so low, you might ask. Well, at 10 to the
minus six torr, a surface picks up about one

121
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:49,980
monolayer per second. At 10 to the minus 11,
one monolayer takes on the order of a day.

122
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:55,960
Then, atomic hydrogen at 330 degrees for
five minutes caps every dangling bond.

123
00:08:56,340 --> 00:09:01,500
That single layer is the resist, inert, where
it's there, reactive, where it's been removed.

124
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:03,560
And why 330?

125
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:09,480
Well, hot enough to get a clean monohydride
layer, but below where the hydrogen leaves.

126
00:09:12,780 --> 00:09:14,260
Now, we write with the tip.

127
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:17,280
STM lithography is controlled tunneling.

128
00:09:17,440 --> 00:09:22,480
The current goes as e to the minus two kappa
d, and kappa is about one per angstrom.

129
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:27,200
So, every angstrom of tip height
changes the current by about e squared.

130
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:31,720
That's seven to eight times. That's
why the tip can see single atoms.

131
00:09:32,200 --> 00:09:34,100
To write, you raise the bias.

132
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:37,060
And the electrons break
silicon hydrogen bonds.

133
00:09:37,480 --> 00:09:40,360
Check the line dose. It's
current divided by speed.

134
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:45,080
10 nano amps over 10 nanometers per
second is one coulomb per meter.

135
00:09:45,540 --> 00:09:48,700
That's the quoted 10 to the minus
two coulombs per centimeter.

136
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:55,040
Arsenic windows use 3.5 volts and 3000
picoamps at 50 nanometers per second.

137
00:09:55,460 --> 00:09:58,400
Two dimers each, 4.6 nanometers apart.

138
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:01,880
You might ask, is this
like e-beam lithography?

139
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:07,020
The answer is yes. Dose is charge per
length, and the resist is one atom thick.

140
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:13,800
Now, dosing, phosphine versus arsine.

141
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:19,140
Dosing is gas kinetics. The flux of molecules
hitting the surface is the pressure over root

142
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:25,520
two pi m k T. For arsine at five times 10 to
the minus nine millibar and room temperature,

143
00:10:25,780 --> 00:10:30,620
that's about nine times 10 to the 11
molecules per square centimeter per second.

144
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:35,440
Over 10 minutes, that's about
0.8 arrivals per surface site.

145
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:40,040
So, each window sees about one molecule,
and the hydrogen resist rejects the rest.

146
00:10:40,540 --> 00:10:43,920
Phosphine loses its hydrogens
step by step over minutes.

147
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:48,040
Arsine breaks apart about a thousand
times faster in tens of seconds.

148
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:52,200
Arsenic coverage maxes out at
about a quarter of the sites.

149
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:55,200
You might ask, why not a full layer?

150
00:10:55,380 --> 00:11:00,840
Well, coverage stops at a quarter, no matter
how the, no matter what the dosage rate is.

151
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:04,040
So, surface chemistry sets
the limit, not the gas supply.

152
00:11:09,580 --> 00:11:12,840
Incorporation, as we know,
needs heat, but not too much.

153
00:11:13,220 --> 00:11:17,580
At 350 degrees, kT is about
54 milli electron volts.

154
00:11:17,980 --> 00:11:23,560
That's enough for the arsenic or phosphorus to swap
into the top silicon layer, but it's below 380,

155
00:11:24,060 --> 00:11:26,600
where hydrogen starts
leaving and the resist fails.

156
00:11:27,440 --> 00:11:31,600
So, a 30-degree window
separates working from failing.

157
00:11:32,160 --> 00:11:37,220
Each arsenic that goes in kicks out
exactly one silicon atom onto the surface.

158
00:11:37,840 --> 00:11:42,740
Arsenic in silicon out. So, counting the
kicked out silicon counts your dopants, right

159
00:11:42,760 --> 00:11:43,760
there in the chamber.

160
00:11:44,860 --> 00:11:51,240
In class terms, this is the drive-in anneal,
which is with basically zero diffusion length.

161
00:11:52,060 --> 00:11:56,620
So, what limits the window? Well, that's the
energy barrier for hydrogen leaving the silicon.

162
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:05,500
Burying the dopants. Now, burying the dopants
is a thermal budget trade-off, good crystal

163
00:12:05,520 --> 00:12:08,940
wants heat, keeping the
atoms in place wants cold.

164
00:12:09,420 --> 00:12:16,220
Phosphorus grown over at 250 degrees drifts
about 5 nanometers up toward the surface.

165
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:21,460
For arsenic, two nanometers grown
unheated to lock the atoms down.

166
00:12:22,060 --> 00:12:31,240
Then a 500-degree anneal for 15
seconds, then 28 nanometers at 250.

167
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:39,560
At 0.003 nanometers per second, those
28 nanometers take about 2.6 hours.

168
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:44,940
Textbook diffusion numbers say that anneal
should barely move anything, but the measured

169
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:47,100
layer is limited by arsenic diffusion.

170
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:51,760
So, at these huge concentrations, the real
diffusivity must be higher than the textbook

171
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:54,980
value. That's why the
anneal is only 15 seconds.

172
00:12:56,140 --> 00:12:59,120
So, you might ask, why not grow it all cold?

173
00:12:59,320 --> 00:13:03,500
Well, cold silicon only grows as a
crystal, up to a critical thickness.

174
00:13:03,880 --> 00:13:06,140
Past that, it ends up being amorphous.

175
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:13,740
Connecting to the buried device is normal
microfab, just with extreme alignment.

176
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:19,740
Etched markers plus a combined STM and SEM, or
Kelvin probe imaging of the buried pattern,

177
00:13:19,960 --> 00:13:24,840
line up the contacts with 95%
yield over more than 150 devices.

178
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:29,580
You can't see the layer optically. It's
a sheet of electrons 30 nanometers down.

179
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:35,760
Hall bars are 20 by 200 microns, made with
optical lithography and reactive ion etching.

180
00:13:36,300 --> 00:13:39,240
Aluminum goes into holes etched
through the doped layer.

181
00:13:39,340 --> 00:13:43,880
Transport is measured at 1.8 Kelvin
and the qubits run at millikelvin.

182
00:13:44,340 --> 00:13:46,980
So, why is the aluminum contact ohmic?

183
00:13:47,240 --> 00:13:51,680
Well, it's because the layer is so heavily
doped that the barrier is thin enough to tunnel

184
00:13:51,700 --> 00:13:52,700
straight through.

185
00:13:57,670 --> 00:14:00,130
Electrical proof uses sheet resistance.

186
00:14:00,690 --> 00:14:06,130
For a 2D sheet, Rs equals
1 over q times Ns times mu.

187
00:14:06,590 --> 00:14:13,490
Measured, 1600 ohms per square, and a mobility
of 28 square centimeters per volt second.

188
00:14:13,890 --> 00:14:19,710
Solve for Ns and you get about 1.4 times
10 to the 14 per square centimeter.

189
00:14:20,050 --> 00:14:25,250
The surface has about 6.8 times 10 to
the 14 atoms per square centimeter.

190
00:14:25,890 --> 00:14:29,350
So that's about one dopant
for every five surface sites.

191
00:14:29,650 --> 00:14:36,730
Activation is 81% or 88%
after a 560 degree anneal.

192
00:14:37,250 --> 00:14:42,170
Photoemission shows the electrons squeezed into
less than a nanometer, and arsenic confines

193
00:14:42,190 --> 00:14:43,870
better than phosphorus.

194
00:14:44,550 --> 00:14:46,290
Why is the mobility so low?

195
00:14:46,770 --> 00:14:51,390
Well, the electrons scatter off the ionized
dopants that they came from, all packed into

196
00:14:51,410 --> 00:14:52,730
one atomic plane.

197
00:14:56,840 --> 00:14:58,240
Two proofs that this works.

198
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:03,220
On the left, single phosphorus atoms
in windows about 1 nanometer across.

199
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:06,780
Three dimers, written at
4 volts and 1 nano amp.

200
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:12,980
Then dosed, annealed, and at 350 and
landing about 12 nanometers apart.

201
00:15:13,540 --> 00:15:17,080
On the right, a transistor whose
island is one phosphorus atom.

202
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:19,460
Its transport shows Coulomb blockade.

203
00:15:19,900 --> 00:15:24,760
The charging energy of a single atom is huge,
so current only flows when a discrete atomic

204
00:15:24,780 --> 00:15:30,440
level lines up with the leads. That gives sharp
conductance peaks as you sweep the gate voltage.

205
00:15:31,480 --> 00:15:37,120
So why does it work at 4 Kelvin? Well, the
single atom charging energy is far above the

206
00:15:37,140 --> 00:15:38,140
thermal energy.

207
00:15:41,690 --> 00:15:43,150
Now here's the catch.

208
00:15:43,730 --> 00:15:45,650
Every site needs to work.

209
00:15:46,110 --> 00:15:48,650
As we know, yield is a
product of probabilities.

210
00:15:49,170 --> 00:15:55,350
If each site works independently with probability
p, an n-site array works with p to the n.

211
00:15:55,930 --> 00:15:56,930
Phosphorus,

212
00:15:58,010 --> 00:16:01,110
in an ideal window, works 63% of the time,

213
00:16:01,550 --> 00:16:03,610
and under 70% in bigger windows.

214
00:16:04,070 --> 00:16:07,610
0.7 to the 16 is about a third of a percent.

215
00:16:08,210 --> 00:16:12,190
For 50 donors, at 63%, it's
about 1 in 10 billion.

216
00:16:12,730 --> 00:16:17,070
Flip it around, the biggest array you can
make half the time is about two sites for

217
00:16:17,090 --> 00:16:21,330
phosphorus and about 23 for arsenic at 97%.

218
00:16:21,350 --> 00:16:24,010
The band shows how sensitive this is.

219
00:16:24,430 --> 00:16:30,030
Arsenic at 95 to 99% gives
44 to 85% for 16 sites.

220
00:16:30,730 --> 00:16:33,290
Now, are the failures really independent?

221
00:16:33,630 --> 00:16:37,590
Well, the formula assumes so. Correlated
failures would change the math.

222
00:16:40,920 --> 00:16:45,620
So what did Dr. Curson do? Well, he
put arsenic in a four-site window.

223
00:16:46,220 --> 00:16:49,700
So, arsenic's fix is a geometry rule.

224
00:16:50,280 --> 00:16:54,380
Arsine breaks apart fast, so one
molecule needs fewer free sites.

225
00:16:54,780 --> 00:16:58,120
A four-site window, two
dimers, takes exactly one.

226
00:16:58,900 --> 00:17:05,440
Single atom yield is 97%, plus or minus two,
with each atom placed within four lattice sites.

227
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:12,820
At 97%, a two-by-two array would come
out perfect, about 89% of the time.

228
00:17:13,260 --> 00:17:17,980
The first perfect two-by-two was actually
made with windows of two to eight sites.

229
00:17:18,560 --> 00:17:21,900
The 97% comes from the
later four-site statistics,

230
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:24,200
and you can retry the failed sites.

231
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:28,840
A second try multiplies the failure
chance, which pushes p toward one.

232
00:17:29,820 --> 00:17:31,040
Why not three sites?

233
00:17:31,720 --> 00:17:36,420
Well, that's because smaller windows let
hydrogen grab the site back before the arsine

234
00:17:36,440 --> 00:17:37,440
actually lands.

235
00:17:41,420 --> 00:17:47,320
So, beyond silicon phosphorus,
new dopants, new hosts.

236
00:17:47,780 --> 00:17:52,840
The same chemistry carries over. On germanium,
arsine goes in at room temperature with no

237
00:17:52,860 --> 00:17:57,920
anneal, and the hydrogen
leaves by 330 degrees.

238
00:17:58,180 --> 00:18:04,260
If a reaction happens on its own at room
temperature, its barrier has to be under about 0.8

239
00:18:04,280 --> 00:18:05,360
electron volts.

240
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:09,760
That comes from an attempt rate
of about 10 to the 13 per second.

241
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:15,860
The rate only stays above once
per second up to about 30 kT.

242
00:18:16,280 --> 00:18:21,540
Bismuth goes the other way. It's a heavy donor
delivered by a molecule called tri-phenyl

243
00:18:21,560 --> 00:18:23,960
bismuth, about a nanometer across.

244
00:18:24,420 --> 00:18:29,560
Its side groups come off near 140
degrees, and bismuth goes in near 360.

245
00:18:30,520 --> 00:18:35,700
That's close to the 380-degree
limit of the hydrogen resist.

246
00:18:36,520 --> 00:18:42,540
So, why germanium? Well, donors there have
bigger Bohr radii and a stronger Stark effect,

247
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:45,240
but this talk at the moment
will stay with silicon.

248
00:18:49,340 --> 00:18:51,440
Now, let's talk about the qubits.

249
00:18:52,120 --> 00:18:58,100
In 2019, an exchange gate between two
donor electrons in about 800 picoseconds,

250
00:18:59,300 --> 00:19:06,120
plus a 3D device with layers aligned better
than 5 nanometers and 97.9% readout.

251
00:19:08,540 --> 00:19:10,860
2025, a 4-qubit processor,

252
00:19:11,360 --> 00:19:18,420
3 phosphorus nuclei sharing 1 electron at 1.45
Tesla and 15 milli-kelvin, with 2-qubit gate

253
00:19:18,440 --> 00:19:27,720
fidelities of 99.3 to 99.65%. And an 11-qubit
processor, 2 registers 13 nanometers apart.

254
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:34,720
The best Bell fidelities are 99.5%
inside a register, and 97% across.

255
00:19:35,620 --> 00:19:38,220
But we need to be careful
when we compare these two.

256
00:19:38,900 --> 00:19:46,220
2019 used natural silicon and electron spins,
whereas 2025 used silicon 28 and nuclear spins.

257
00:19:46,860 --> 00:19:48,240
Now, why nuclear spins?

258
00:19:48,700 --> 00:19:51,120
Well, they just hold their
coherence much longer.

259
00:19:54,760 --> 00:19:56,880
So, here's the physics in one equation.

260
00:19:57,260 --> 00:20:02,140
Three terms, electron Zeeman,
nuclear Zeeman, and hyperfine.

261
00:20:02,560 --> 00:20:06,360
Three nuclei with hyperfine values of 6, 68,

262
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:13,640
and 103 megahertz. Each one shifts the electron's
frequency up or down by half its value.

263
00:20:14,020 --> 00:20:17,500
Two to the three gives eight ESR lines.

264
00:20:18,020 --> 00:20:22,780
Check. The full spread is 6 plus 68 plus 103.

265
00:20:23,460 --> 00:20:28,860
That's 177 megahertz, exactly
the gap between the outer lines.

266
00:20:29,360 --> 00:20:32,480
So, the electron's frequency
reads out the nuclear register.

267
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:34,620
Now, let's do a thermal check.

268
00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:37,960
The electron sits near 200 milli-kelvin.

269
00:20:38,300 --> 00:20:42,520
hf at 39 gigahertz is about 9 times kT.

270
00:20:43,140 --> 00:20:46,020
So, the electron is still
almost fully polarized.

271
00:20:46,520 --> 00:20:53,260
Coherence, about 28 microseconds for the
electron, and half a millisecond to 1.3

272
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:54,960
milliseconds for the nuclei.

273
00:20:55,620 --> 00:20:59,760
Now, why does the hyperfine
change with electric field?

274
00:21:00,960 --> 00:21:06,340
Well, gate voltages reshape the
electron wave function at the nucleus.

275
00:21:10,810 --> 00:21:13,550
Entanglement is checked with state tomography.

276
00:21:14,210 --> 00:21:17,430
You measure in lots of bases
and rebuild the density matrix.

277
00:21:18,010 --> 00:21:23,230
For a perfect Bell state, the matrix has four
equal corners of one half and nothing else.

278
00:21:23,950 --> 00:21:26,310
Fidelity is the overlap with that ideal.

279
00:21:26,850 --> 00:21:31,830
All three nuclear pairs hit 96.8 to 97.7%.

280
00:21:31,850 --> 00:21:37,790
The GHZ state ties all three
nuclei together at 96.2%.

281
00:21:37,810 --> 00:21:42,470
Then, Grover's search on three
qubits with eight possible answers.

282
00:21:42,890 --> 00:21:46,550
The ideal success after two rounds is 94.5%.

283
00:21:46,570 --> 00:21:52,710
The hardware got 93.5. That's 98.9% of ideal.

284
00:21:53,210 --> 00:21:54,210
Now,

285
00:21:54,330 --> 00:21:59,470
why are there only two rounds? Well, a third
round would overshoot the answer and it would

286
00:21:59,490 --> 00:22:00,510
lower the probability.

287
00:22:04,700 --> 00:22:08,040
Now, let's look at the same atoms,

288
00:22:08,620 --> 00:22:12,500
but a new job and a
quantum simulator to match.

289
00:22:13,060 --> 00:22:15,960
Dopant arrays can also
simulate many body physics.

290
00:22:16,380 --> 00:22:21,560
The Hubbard model has two energies, hopping,
called T, set by tunneling between the dots

291
00:22:21,580 --> 00:22:24,540
and on-site repulsion, called U.

292
00:22:24,960 --> 00:22:27,500
Hopping drops exponentially with spacing.

293
00:22:28,060 --> 00:22:36,240
So, stretching a 3x3 lattice from 4.1 to
10.7 nanometers takes U over T from small,

294
00:22:36,800 --> 00:22:40,360
which is a metal, to large,
which is a Mott insulator,

295
00:22:40,800 --> 00:22:43,200
where repulsion freezes
the electrons in place.

296
00:22:43,720 --> 00:22:47,520
A 10-dot chain with alternating
couplings builds the SSH model.

297
00:22:48,020 --> 00:22:52,300
When the hopping inside each cell is weaker
than the hopping between cells, the chain is

298
00:22:52,320 --> 00:22:53,320
topological,

299
00:22:53,440 --> 00:22:55,420
with edge states at zero energy.

300
00:22:56,340 --> 00:23:00,060
You see two conductance peaks,
essentially, instead of 10.

301
00:23:01,040 --> 00:23:02,880
So, why use dopants for this?

302
00:23:03,540 --> 00:23:06,120
Lithographic precision
sets the hopping directly.

303
00:23:11,340 --> 00:23:14,480
Throughput, as we mentioned,
is a scaling problem.

304
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:18,560
In precise mode, a tip writes
about 100 atoms per second.

305
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:23,800
One square centimeter of silicon has about
seven times 10 to the 14 surface sites.

306
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:30,700
Writing that one atom at a time
takes about 215,000 years.

307
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:36,400
At two volts and hundreds of nano amps, the
tip goes 1,000 nanometers per second and still

308
00:23:36,420 --> 00:23:37,700
keeps its resolution.

309
00:23:38,280 --> 00:23:40,980
Precision is only lost in field emission mode.

310
00:23:41,360 --> 00:23:46,080
EUV lithography exposes the equivalent
of 10 to the 14 nanometers per second.

311
00:23:46,440 --> 00:23:52,120
That's 11 to 13 orders of magnitude faster
because every photon exposes in parallel.

312
00:23:52,760 --> 00:23:57,160
Even 10 million tips across a
wafer would still be way too slow.

313
00:23:58,040 --> 00:24:00,360
Can tips just go faster, you might ask?

314
00:24:00,380 --> 00:24:04,540
Well, speed will trade
directly against precision.

315
00:24:09,940 --> 00:24:13,260
EUV can strip the same hydrogen resist.

316
00:24:13,780 --> 00:24:16,400
Photon energy is hc over lambda.

317
00:24:17,400 --> 00:24:23,480
Industrial 13 and a half nanometer light is
about 92 electron volts and clear hydrogen

318
00:24:23,500 --> 00:24:26,840
removal showed up at 93 and 106.

319
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:29,300
It works indirectly.

320
00:24:29,960 --> 00:24:35,020
The silicon absorbs the photons and spits out
secondary electrons and those electrons break

321
00:24:35,040 --> 00:24:36,600
the silicon hydrogen bonds.

322
00:24:37,020 --> 00:24:38,760
Same physics as under the tip.

323
00:24:39,160 --> 00:24:44,700
The cost is dose, up to 18,000 joules per
square centimeter, which means exposures on the

324
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:47,020
order of minutes with 300 watt sources.

325
00:24:47,900 --> 00:24:54,600
The plan is essentially EUV for the leads
and wiring, STM for the qubit cores.

326
00:24:55,740 --> 00:25:00,580
What's the resolution? Well, diffraction
limits EUV to roughly about 10 nanometers.

327
00:25:04,380 --> 00:25:08,140
Our prototype applies these
design rules with real numbers.

328
00:25:08,580 --> 00:25:13,680
Four arsenic atoms, one per four-site
window, 4.6 nanometers apart.

329
00:25:14,300 --> 00:25:18,760
A single electron transistor 17 nanometers
away for readout and two gates.

330
00:25:19,200 --> 00:25:24,040
All of it made of doped arsenic in
one plane, buried 30 nanometers deep.

331
00:25:24,460 --> 00:25:29,340
Expected yield is about 0.97
to the fourth, about 89%.

332
00:25:29,360 --> 00:25:36,120
Scale up to 16 sites and it's 61% for
arsenic versus 0.3% for phosphorus.

333
00:25:36,140 --> 00:25:43,460
So how big a register can you build is set by
the chemistry through p, not by lithography.

334
00:25:44,580 --> 00:25:47,640
What sets the 17 nanometer
spacing you might ask?

335
00:25:47,900 --> 00:25:51,720
Well, that's sensor sensitivity
versus tunnel coupling.

336
00:25:51,920 --> 00:25:53,580
It's an exponential tradeoff.

337
00:25:57,220 --> 00:25:59,000
Here's the traveler as numbers.

338
00:25:59,640 --> 00:26:01,480
Let's look at the hot steps first.

339
00:26:02,060 --> 00:26:05,320
A 1200 degree flash and a 600 degree out gas.

340
00:26:05,720 --> 00:26:10,720
After patterning, the hottest step is
a 500 degree anneal for 15 seconds.

341
00:26:11,160 --> 00:26:15,700
Then 350 for incorporation
and 250 for overgrowth.

342
00:26:16,220 --> 00:26:21,100
Why that order works is because diffusion
goes up exponentially with temperature.

343
00:26:21,620 --> 00:26:26,620
So that short 500 degree step
dominates everything after patterning.

344
00:26:26,680 --> 00:26:28,660
And it sets the final width.

345
00:26:29,100 --> 00:26:35,000
Compare that with our own lab, a 1050 degree
drive-in for about 40 minutes moves phosphorus

346
00:26:35,020 --> 00:26:36,420
hundreds of nanometers.

347
00:26:36,900 --> 00:26:39,260
It's the same physics, but the opposite goal.

348
00:26:40,060 --> 00:26:41,520
Where is the risk?

349
00:26:42,020 --> 00:26:48,060
Well, the risk is in the anneal because if it's too
hot or too long, then the layer will spread out.

350
00:26:53,300 --> 00:26:55,440
So here's the key takeaway.

351
00:26:56,320 --> 00:27:00,140
There are actually three. So one is precision.

352
00:27:00,700 --> 00:27:06,880
Placement is set by the
lattice 0.38 nanometers.

353
00:27:07,040 --> 00:27:10,040
And exchange depends exponentially on spacing.

354
00:27:11,020 --> 00:27:13,460
So precision is a must.

355
00:27:13,980 --> 00:27:14,980
Yield.

356
00:27:15,220 --> 00:27:17,480
Arrays work with p to the n.

357
00:27:18,140 --> 00:27:25,380
Arsenic pushed p to 97%, which moves the
half-yield array size from about two sites to

358
00:27:25,400 --> 00:27:27,000
about 23 sites.

359
00:27:28,400 --> 00:27:29,460
Throughput.

360
00:27:29,880 --> 00:27:34,460
Writing a hundred atoms per
second can't cover a wafer.

361
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:38,680
So parallel light on the same
hydrogen resist is the bridge.

362
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:41,300
And our quantum tests passed.

363
00:27:41,900 --> 00:27:49,200
An eight-line spectrum Bell states
near 97% and Grover at 98.9% of ideal.

364
00:27:50,580 --> 00:27:53,020
So you might ask what comes next?

365
00:27:53,320 --> 00:27:54,840
Well, bigger registers,

366
00:27:55,320 --> 00:27:58,340
which is exactly the p to the n problem.

367
00:28:00,820 --> 00:28:05,900
And I have some references as well and

368
00:28:07,990 --> 00:28:09,890
some titles

369
00:28:11,220 --> 00:28:16,980
here. So this is a Monte
Carlo check of p to the n.

370
00:28:17,060 --> 00:28:21,660
So each simulated array fills 16
sites with independent coin flips.

371
00:28:22,200 --> 00:28:26,040
And the model in this case
assumes sites fail independently.

372
00:28:26,340 --> 00:28:31,580
So if you have clustered defects, like let's
say you have a dirty patch, then that would

373
00:28:31,600 --> 00:28:34,720
make big arrays fail more
often than p to the n predicts.

374
00:28:35,500 --> 00:28:37,960
So feel free to use that.

375
00:28:39,180 --> 00:28:40,820
Thank you very much.
